"The patient underwent a robot-assisted radical prostatectomy with a pathological stage of pT4b, positive surgical margins, seminal vesicle invasion, and lymph node metastasis. Preoperative findings included a high preoperative PSA of 86. The surgical findings included a Gleason 4+3 (ISUP grade group 3). The biopsy demonstrated a higher grade, with a Gleason 4+4 (ISUP grade group 4). The presence of lymph node metastasis was a significant factor in the prognosis. The positive surgical margins and seminal vesicle invasion are also concerning features.\n\nCountervailing factors include the absence of lymphovascular invasion and the absence of lymph node metastasis was noted in the surgical report, although nodal metastasis was documented. The difference between the biopsy grade (Gleason 4+4) and the prostatectomy grade (Gleason 4+3) is a documented finding. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data currently widens the uncertainty regarding the trajectory. Information regarding postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory would be crucial to revise the prognosis. The heuristic horizon suggests a time frame for recurrence. The fixed estimated horizon is 29.84 months. This is a predicted recurrence horizon, not evidence of observed recurrence. The sensitivity range is 23.87–35.81 months; its coverage has not been validated."
